 - **reed** — **the window to the world**: tmux overlay + **strand** bookkeeping + render (`internal/reedcli` + `internal/reedengine` + `internal/reedengine/render`). Hosts every managed process as a strand in a window of its own or its group's, persists to `.lym/reed.json` (`lym reed up|add|remove|status|list|attach|switch|resume|watchdog|down`); `lym reed add` places a strand `--anchor own-window` by default. Every strand carries a hierarchical full name (see the `internal/agentname` package documentation), mirrored into its pane title and Claude session name and exported to its process as `LYM_STRAND_NAME` and `LYM_PARENT`, with the watchdog repairing drift; `list` is the hub-wide name directory. Built on what its proof-of-concept, `muxpoc`, proved first (env hygiene, native `--resume`); `muxpoc` has since been deleted, its job done. `reed attach` and `reed watchdog` are this module's two registered interactive-handoff exceptions ([PATTERN-cli-cobra](../pattern/PATTERN-cli-cobra.md)): `attach` hands the operator's stdio to a `tmux attach-session` child in place, pre-flighting through checking tmux's floor version, booting the session if cold and checking server/session liveness, and `watchdog` is the detached per-hub name-repair and reap daemon that blocks for its process lifetime — in both cases every fallible step runs pre-flight, on the envelope, and only the terminal-handover/daemon tail itself is exempt from emitting JSON. `add` and `attach` boot this worktree's session when none is up, with `up`'s own semantics — a bare substrate, with no persisted strand relaunched — instead of refusing; every other reed verb still refuses on a cold worktree. ✅ Implemented. See the `internal/reedengine` package documentation.
-- **shuttle** — run **one** LLM agent as an interactive tmux strand over the file contract (`internal/shuttleengine` + `internal/shuttleengine/claudeengine` + `internal/shuttleengine/codexengine` + `internal/shuttleengines` + `internal/shuttlecli`; `lym shuttle run|open|state|await|await-forks`). `lym shuttle open` starts a named session with no turn in its own window, which closes when the session exits; `--model` takes a model spec naming the engine, model and effort, `--list-models` prints the model registry's aliases with their engine, model and default effort, and the output names the engine, model and effort the session got. Every session shuttle launches, run or opened, starts in its engine's bypass mode unless the caller passes `prompt`, which an interactive session accepts. `lym shuttle await <path>... [--cap]` is the read-only capped wait on one or more files, the keep-cache-warm wait any agent can run, needing no hub or git repository; its cap defaults to the caller role's keep-warm cap from `claude.yaml`. `lym shuttle await-forks [--cap]` is the same capped wait over the forks the calling agent session spawned, read from its transcript through the engine: a reviewer, fixer or writer that waits inside it takes every fork's result in one turn instead of waking once per fork. `lym shuttle state` is read-only: it lists the session state of each running run (busy, idle-done, idle-stalled, idle-fresh, asking, dead or unknown, with its cause, since and history), read from the run's files, in shadow mode where no stop or notice depends on it. `Stop`-hook completion is read off an events file and classified into three outcomes — `done`/`died`/`timeout`. A turn end without every output file never ends a run: `Wait` holds it and keeps polling the same agent until the outputs exist, the pane dies or the deadline passes, whatever the agent's reason (a question, a status report, a live `AskUserQuestion` call). An autonomous run notifies its parent once per held turn end as creel mail to the orch, and once per permission or input dialog its pane shows past 60 s, and an interactive run is answered in its pane; a line typed into a run's pane goes through `lym creel post`, `lym creel inject` or `lym creel interrupt`, never a shuttle verb. Background work is read off the Stop payload's `background_tasks` first, with the transcript as the fallback when the payload carries no list; a turn end with outstanding work is waiting, not held, unless its only outstanding work is non-awaited shells, which a gated run holds at once with an output file missing, a gated run with every output file present counts done once they pass the shell wait bound after telling the agent to read them, and an ungated run holds once they pass the shell wait bound. `Attach` waits on a held run, and on a legacy `asking` record whose strand is still live, instead of respawning beside it. `died` is narrow: it means a strand reed STILL TRACKS has a pane that is not alive, the provider never came up inside the startup window, or the provider's process is proven gone behind a live pane shell (by the process registry, or a process-ending `SessionEnd` line past the wait's first liveness tick), with `done` instead when every output file exists. Reed's strand table losing the strand entirely — a `reed down`/`remove`, a deleted or rebuilt `reed.json`, a worktree renamed under an in-flight run — is reported as a mechanism failure carrying the run's identity, never as `died`, because reed's bookkeeping going away says nothing about the agent, whose process is often still working in its pane; the exception is a handle whose own `Stop` removed the strand, which ends `died` and is recorded as stopped unless the files show the run done. No guardrail denies the in-process `Agent` tool: a subagent or fork shows in the session's own pane and lym reads its transcript under the session's `subagents` directory. A `PreToolUse` guardrail denies `AskUserQuestion` when the run is autonomous (`Interactive: false`, the default); a `PreToolUse(Bash)` guardrail denies `python`, `python3` and `python3.<minor>` in command position in every run mode, a version check included (on by default, switchable off via `shuttle.yaml`'s `deny_python`), and a second `PreToolUse(Bash)` guardrail, installed in every run mode with no switch, denies a raw module-wide `go test`, `go build` or `go vet`, a raw `go test` over the `tmux` or `llm` tier, and any command naming the gate-slot or strand-name environment variable, steering the session to `lym gate test`, and to a package-scoped compile check the hub's `refuse_raw` does not refuse, and a third `PreToolUse(Bash)` guardrail, installed in every run mode with no switch, denies an `rm` on a computed path (a command substitution or a variable expansion in the command) so no permission dialog stalls the run, and a fourth `PreToolUse(Bash)` guardrail, installed in every run mode with no switch, denies `sed -i`, `perl -i`, and a redirect, heredoc or `tee` into a source file, steering the session to read a file with Read and change it with Edit or Write, and a fifth `PreToolUse(Bash)` guardrail, installed in every run mode with no switch, denies a headless `claude` launch with `-p` or `--print`, whose work shows in no pane, steering the session to a subagent through the `Agent` tool; the python deny's steer carries the same edit rule. The engine announces each deny it installs to the session's system prompt, and every Claude session it launches with a run directory also gets the creel trust sentence, through one `append-system-prompt.txt` file named by `--append-system-prompt-file` on launch and resume, so a session knows the tool is unavailable before it tries one and follows `[creel]` mail from its hooks. The engine sets the prompt-cache TTL per role on launch and resume from `claude.yaml`'s `prompt_cache_ttl` and `prompt_cache_ttl_roles` (template: 5 minutes for `driver`, 1 hour for `webster` and `orch`, 5 minutes for every